# Scoped NOPASSWD sudoers rules for markw's windows-auto-build-pipeline project.
#
# Grants passwordless root for exactly the disk-prep tooling this project's
# offline Windows image-apply pipeline needs: attaching a qcow2 via qemu-nbd,
# partitioning/formatting the resulting nbd block device, and mounting the
# two filesystem types this project uses (vfat for the ESP, ntfs-3g for the
# Windows volume). Nothing else - no shell, no su, no package management,
# no systemctl, no arbitrary command execution.
#
# SCOPE NOTE, read before installing: sudoers command matching is a glob-
# string match against the literal command line, not a true per-argument
# ACL. The rules below restrict *which binaries* can run passwordless (the
# real security value here) and, where each tool's CLI convention puts the
# device path in a fixed position, restrict it to /dev/nbd* devices only
# (never a real host disk). For qemu-nbd's own file argument and mount's
# option string, full flexibility remains after the pinned prefix - e.g.
# qemu-nbd could in principle attach a file outside this project's own
# directories. This is an appropriate tradeoff for a single-user lab/dev
# host, not a hardened multi-tenant security boundary. Wimapply, bcd-sys.sh,
# and the hivex tools are deliberately NOT in this file - they run as the
# normal user against nbd-mounted partitions created with uid=/gid= mount
# options, so they never need root at all.
#
# Update: BCD-SYS (tools/vendor/BCD-SYS/Linux/bcd-sys.sh) internally shells
# out to `sudo sfdisk -l <disk>` (read-only partition-table query) to
# confirm the ESP's disk uses a GPT/DOS scheme, when invoked in our exact
# shape - a plain mounted-directory source plus `-s <esp-mountpoint>` for
# UEFI firmware. Traced the script directly rather than guessing: in this
# invocation shape it does NOT reach its efibootmgr/NVRAM-entry code (that
# path is explicitly skipped whenever --syspath is used, confirmed by
# reading the source) and does not reach its own mount/unmount helpers
# either (those are gated on flags that are only set when BCD-SYS mounted
# the ESP itself, not when handed an already-mounted path via -s, which is
# our case). So `sfdisk -l` is the only addition needed for this tool.
#
# Update 2: BCD-SYS's Linux/update_device.sh (used to compute the BCD's
# device-locator element pointing at the Windows partition) separately
# calls `sudo sfdisk -o Device,UUID -l <disk>` - a different flag shape
# than plain `sfdisk -l <disk>` above, so it did NOT match the existing
# rule and was silently failing every run. This is a read-only listing
# mode regardless of which columns are requested (sfdisk only mutates a
# partition table when given a script on stdin, never in `-l`/list mode),
# so widening the pinned suffix to match any `-o <columns> -l <disk>`
# invocation carries the same read-only safety property as the original
# rule, not a materially bigger grant.
#
# Update 3: added for the NTFS $LogFile/dirty-bit investigation flagged
# back in PHASE3_ENGINEERING_LOG.md Session 3 as a real, not-yet-tested
# hypothesis behind Windows 11's post-Sysprep BSOD (Session 4/Finding 12) -
# ntfsinfo (read-only volume/journal inspection) and ntfsfix (resets the
# NTFS journal file and schedules a native Windows chkdsk at next mount;
# does not itself repair file/directory data) both need to run against the
# nbd-attached partition device directly (not the ntfs-3g mount point uid=/
# gid= options already make writable as the normal user), so both need
# root the same way mkntfs/sgdisk already do. Pinned to /dev/nbd[0-9]*
# exactly like every other rule in this file - never a real host disk.
# ntfsinfo takes no device-modifying flags at all; ntfsfix's options are
# limited to -b/-d/-n/-h/-V (see its own man page) - none of them can
# target a path other than the pinned device argument.
#
# Update 4 (2026-08-24): reverses the "wimapply...never need[s] root at all"
# claim in this file's own header - PHASE3_ENGINEERING_LOG.md's "ROOT CAUSE
# CONFIRMED" entry traced the Start Menu/DCOM-activation crash directly to
# that exact design choice. `wimlib-imagex apply` against an ntfs-3g mount
# opened with uid=/gid= (this project's only mount mode, chosen specifically
# so wimapply could run as the normal user) silently drops every file's real
# Windows security descriptor - confirmed directly by re-running the same
# apply with wimlib's own `--strict-acls` flag, which fails immediately with
# "Extraction backend does not support security descriptors!" wimlib has a
# separate, working code path for this: pointing `apply` at the raw nbd
# partition device instead of a FUSE-mounted directory invokes its own
# built-in direct-NTFS-volume writer (confirmed empirically - distinct log
# output, "Applying image ... to NTFS volume /dev/nbd0p3" instead of the
# FUSE-mount run's FILE_ATTRIBUTE_* warnings), which needs to open the block
# device directly and therefore needs root the same way mkntfs/sgdisk do.
# `--strict-acls` is pinned as a **required literal suffix**, not left
# wildcard-open like most other rules' trailing flags - the whole point of
# this rule is to guarantee any future silent regression back to dropped
# ACLs fails loudly instead of quietly reintroducing this exact bug. The WIM
# path is pinned to this project's own wim-cache directory (never an
# arbitrary file); the OS subfolder and image index are left open since
# they're expected to vary per build and are validated by validate_os()/
# os_wim_index() in image-apply/lib/common.sh before this ever runs, not by
# sudoers itself. Not yet confirmed whether the direct-NTFS-volume apply
# mode actually produces correct ACLs when run as root - this rule only
# grants the permission needed to test that; the pipeline scripts themselves
# (image-apply/apply-image.sh) have not been changed to use it yet.

markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/sfdisk -o * -l /dev/nbd[0-9]*
#
# Install (do not just `cp` this into place - validate first):
#   visudo -cf /home/markw/Documents/Projects/windows-auto-build-pipeline/tools/sudoers-windows-auto-build-pipeline
#   sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root \
#     /home/markw/Documents/Projects/windows-auto-build-pipeline/tools/sudoers-windows-auto-build-pipeline \
#     /etc/sudoers.d/windows-auto-build-pipeline
#   sudo visudo -c

markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/modprobe nbd max_part=8
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/modprobe -r nbd

markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/qemu-nbd -c /dev/nbd[0-9]* *
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/qemu-nbd -d /dev/nbd[0-9]*

markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/sgdisk * /dev/nbd[0-9]*
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/partprobe /dev/nbd[0-9]*

markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/mkfs.vfat * /dev/nbd[0-9]*
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/mkntfs * /dev/nbd[0-9]*
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/mkfs.ntfs * /dev/nbd[0-9]*

markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/mount -t vfat -o * /dev/nbd[0-9]* /tmp/win-build-mnt/*
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/mount -t ntfs-3g -o * /dev/nbd[0-9]* /tmp/win-build-mnt/*
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/umount /tmp/win-build-mnt/*
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/umount /dev/nbd[0-9]*

markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/sfdisk -l /dev/nbd[0-9]*

markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ntfsinfo * /dev/nbd[0-9]*
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ntfsfix * /dev/nbd[0-9]*
# ntfsfix's most basic invocation (no flags, just the device) has only a
# single space between the binary and its argument - the rule above
# requires two (one on each side of the `*`), so it doesn't match a bare
# call the same way `qemu-nbd -r -c ...` didn't match the -c-only rule
# elsewhere in this file. Same fix as that gotcha: an explicit bare-form
# rule alongside the flagged one, rather than forcing every caller to
# always pass a flag just to satisfy the glob.
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ntfsfix /dev/nbd[0-9]*

# See "Update 4" above. --strict-acls is a required literal suffix, not a
# wildcard-open trailing flag - by design.
markw ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/wimlib-imagex apply /home/markw/Documents/Projects/windows-builds/windows-auto-build-pipeline/image-apply/output/wim-cache/*/install.wim * /dev/nbd[0-9]*p3 --strict-acls
